   10-13, 2004},
Organization = {NIDCD; Psi Iota Xi; Indiana Lions; St Joseph Inst Deaf; Indiana Univ
   Cochlear Implant Team},
Abstract = {The candidature criteria for cochlear implantation have been expanded to
   allow some special cases of pre- or perilingual deafness to be
   considered for implantation. In this study, the adaptation of this
   borderline group to the new HiRes speech coding strategy was of
   particular interest. A group of 10 pre- or perilingual deaf subjects
   were selected. The subjects were switched on in standard mode (CIS or
   SAS). Following between 6 months and 2 years of implant experience, all
   were converted to the HiRes Mode. Speech perception was evaluated using
   the Freiburger Numbers and Monosyllables test and the HSM sentence test
   in quiet and in noise. Baseline testing was conducted immediately prior
   to HiRes fitting using the standard mode and repeated after 1-month
   exposure to HiRes. In addition a questionnaire examining the sound
   quality was administered. The majority of this group obtained benefit
   through conversion to HiRes. Nine out of 10 subjects have a strong
   preference for the HiRes mode. In some cases, several iterations were
   necessary to optimise the HiRes program. Since conversion to HiRes,
   three subjects have been able to obtain a reasonably high score in the
   HSM sentence test in noise. (c) 2004 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.},
