   REPRESENTATION AND MULTIPLE USES, BRNO, CZECHOSLOVAKIA, APR 22-25, 1991},
Organization = {INT GEOG UNION},
Abstract = {Geographic Information Systems (GIS) have become of increasing
   significance for environmental planning and assessment in recent years.
   One main reason for this is the need, in Environmental Planning, to
   compare a great number of area-related data describing the affected
   natural resources and their sensitivity related to the effects of
   impacts. Because GIS can be used to couple area-related data with their
   attributes, and can be used to overlay these, it represents a highly
   efficient instrument for such planning tasks.
   This paper describes some of the basic application methods by using a
   Geographic Information System. Project work and GIS application in
   connection with model-appoaches are demonstrated with several examples
   from existing project work: (a) MAB-project 6 - Ecosystem Research
   Berchtesgaden; (b) Environmental Impact Assessment Study for the Danube
   River construction works between Straubing and Vilshofen; (c)
   Determination of Noise Pollution Effects on Residents near the new
   airport Munich II; (d) Environmental Impact Assessment for the Federal
   Motorway A94.
   The chosen examples from research and planning projects have been worked
   out by using GIS and environmental modelling capabilities. For every
   project a different GIS database was created and evaluated},
