Title = {Transjurisdictional Water Pollution Disputes and Measures of Resolution
   -Taking the Yellow River Basin as an Example},
Booktitle = {PROCEEDINGS OF THE 1ST INTERNATIONAL YELLOW RIVER FORUM ON RIVER BASIN
   MANAGEMENT, VOL IV},
Year = {2003},
Pages = {290-297},
Note = {1st International Yellow River Forum, Zhengzhou, PEOPLES R CHINA, MAY
   12-15, 2003},
Organization = {Yellow River Conservancy Commiss; Minist Water Resources; China Int Ctr
   Econ \& Tech Exchange; Xiaolangdi Multiple Purpose Project Construct \&
   Adm Bur; Natl Nat Sci Fdn China; NPC, Legislat Dept Nat \& Environm
   Resources Comm},
Abstract = {Transjurisdictional water pollution problems are becoming more and more
   noticeable in China. A number of transjurisdictional water pollution
   incidents with important transjurisdictional effects have already
   occurred. A large number of transjurisdictional water pollution disputes
   which have far - reaching social effects have also occurred between
   Provinces, including the Yellow River Basin. Furthermore, within
   provinces, various transjurisdictional water pollution disputes are also
   frequent between municipalities, counties towns and villages.
   In this paper, the authors introduced and analyzed several
   transjurisdictional water pollution cases in China, especially in the
   Yellow River Basin. The problems and causes for transjurisdictional
   water pollution were addressed in the paper. It was pointed out that
   there are many causes for transjurisdictional water pollution problems
   although the key reasons are the rapid economic development, the failure
   of local authorities to enforce the existing laws and to implement
   administrative decisions, as well as the lack of a feasible legal
   system. Other factors were also recognized such as the poor technology,
   shortage of funds, the contrast between the division of jurisdiction and
   the continuity of water resources in space, the unreasonable division of
   water quantity and quality, both administratively and legally, the lack
   of transparency in the decision - making process, insufficient public
   participation and information disclosure I poor communication, and lack
   of involvement of stakeholders in basin or sub - basin organizations.
   Measures for resolution of transjurisdictional water pollution problems
   were also raised in the paper.},
