   KOREA, AUG 25-29, 2024},
Abstract = {Continuous and safe process operation, along with higher profits,
   credibility and reputation are fuel for success in any process industry.
   Human factor remains pivotal in achieving these objectives. Human error
   has become a necessary concept within safety frameworks. Without
   thoroughly exploring the recent literature on human errors in the
   process industry, it is unwise to develop strategies for improving human
   performance or reduce human error in the future. Therefore, this study
   conducts a literature review from 2013 to 2023 by examining how concept
   of human error has advanced, its interpretation, and demographic factors
   like geography and orientation by discipline are assessed to provide a
   complete holistic view. Furthermore, the definition of human error and
   methods used across this industry are also investigated in the
   literature. Findings from the review showed a varied collection of
   methodologies for investigating human error, including systematic and
   cognitive approaches. Interpretations of human error vary, with some
   studies emphasizing individual mistakes while others highlight systemic
   issues. Additionally, demographic factors such as geography and
   disciplinary orientation influence the perception and characterization
   of human error within process industries. The insights from this work
   lay a foundation for future research and the development of specific
   methods to improve human performance, encouraging higher safety in
   process industry and other socio-technical systems (e.g., maritime,
   healthcare and chemical).},
