Note = {International Multidisciplinary Scientific Conferences on Social
   Sciences and Arts (SGEM 2014), Albena, BULGARIA, SEP 01-10, 2014},
Organization = {Bulgarian Acad Sci; Acad Sci Czech Republ; Latvian Acad Sci; Polish Acad
   Sci; Russian Acad Sci; Serbian Acad Sci \& Arts; Slovak Acad Sci Intro;
   Natl Acad Sci Ukraine; Natl Acad Sci Armenia; Sci Counc Japan; World
   Acad Sci TWAS; European Acad Sci, Arts \& Lett; Acad Fine Arts Zagreb
   Croatia; Croatian Acad Sci \& Arts; Acad Sci Moldova; Montenegrin Acad
   Sci \& Arts; Georgian Acad Sci; Acad Fine Arts \& Design Bratislava;
   Russian Acad Arts; Turkish Acad Sci; SGEM},
Abstract = {The purpose of the paper was to investigate the idea of
   intergenerational dialogue as a toollcit, beneficial for societies that
   recognised their need for adapting the ``all ages{''} perspective. Such
   an outlook is hitherto a case of an ideal social structure type, yet
   Poland and other European countries already experience the need for
   flexibility of social roles available throughout entire life cycle,
   essential in the discussed structure type. By desk research the paper
   addressed steps to be undertaken in order to achieve a structure
   characterised as age integrated and structural lag free. I examined
   changes enabling its synchronisation with population ageing processes.
   And most of all the scheme resilience still attributing the ``young{''}
   solely to educational fields, the ``middle-aged{''} to labour market
   area and the ``old{''} to severely limited social roles available on
   retirement. Major findings of the paper provided a broader discourse on
   challenges of intergenerational dialogue and were to have a positive
   impact on practical after-effects as well as to extend the field
   apprehension in several fields such as:
   - acquiring perception of intergenerational dialogue as a toolkit for
   generational independence, autonomy and interdependence, the latter
   being perhaps the first-rate bridge to self-constitution and sense of
   subjectivity in treating oneself and age-others
   - advancing social institutions' responsiveness in terms of various
   generations' demands
   -developing a foundation for new forms of intergenerational relations.},
